WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.450
We're here with Brad and Rachel, a happily married couple,

2
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.390
and we're talking to them about having

3
00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.160
different parenting styles for their three children.

4
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.220
And I'm here with my good friend and parenting expert

5
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.690
and child behavioralist Dr. Elisa Pressman.

6
00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.900
When we went out of commercial break,

7
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.060
one of the things that we were talking about,

8
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.710
was that you guys have different styles.

9
00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.090
And one of the things I found,

10
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:29.090
is such a pitfall is the weak have to have a united front.

11
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.540
And what's so difficult about that

12
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:34.480
is you are two individuals who are expected

13
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.110
to become one person, one mentality.

14
00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:42.490
And I find that the difficult thing about that is

15
00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.440
if you try to conform to someone else,

16
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.630
eventually you will lose yourself.

17
00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.240
And I believe it is human behavior

18
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.190
that we will inevitably fight to become ourselves again,

19
00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.010
which usually means we're gonna have to distance

20
00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.160
from the other person.

21
00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.590
So if you guys want to stay connected,

22
00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.230
is there ever gonna be a day

23
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.500
where you guys can get to a place where you celebrate

24
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.040
each other's different styles?

25
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.360
I think that's a really great way to look at it.

26
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.710
I don't think before we,

27
00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.240
have been speaking with you that that was something

28
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.230
that we felt like was even an option.

29
00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:17.920
Looking at it from that perspective

30
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.230
and him being one style and me being another,

31
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:21.700
and that being okay,

32
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.990
I think is probably the only way to move forward without

33
00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.830
eventually going separate ways.

34
00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:28.430
Yeah, I think that's a great point.

35
00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.330
Elisa, what do you think?

36
00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.370
What a great place to come to

37
00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.400
to honor the fact that you each come from different places

38
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.250 line:15%
and there's a benefit

39
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.090 line:15%
to knowing that Mom is a particular way

40
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.610
and Dad is a particular way.

41
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.680
Kids are very smart and they'll just kind of learn

42
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.150
who to go to for certain things.

43
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.400
And that's totally okay.

44
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.620
It's funny, 'cause sometimes Elisa,

45
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.250
we did like a design thing together recently

46
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.620
and she was talking about how kids,

47
00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.703
like have a literally a different eye perspective.

48
00:01:59.201 --> 00:02:03.380
Who knows if your kids see you Rachel, as the bad cop.

49
00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:06.440
And they may look at their dad and see this

50
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:10.030
very bigger than life figure and be slightly intimidated.

51
00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.410
And you might be this cozy, safe person.

52
00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.100
And Elisa is right.

53
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.450
If you lay down the law

54
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.620
and you are full of their boundaries,

55
00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.410
it does not drive your children away.

56
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:23.360
They do actually feel a sense of safety

57
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.290
from those boundaries,

58
00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.210
which I was terrified of for years

59
00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.350
that it turns out it's true!

60
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.590
Yeah, that's really something for me

61
00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.150
to grab a hold to and take comfort in.

62
00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.030
Rachel, what is like,

63
00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.090
ideally your favorite healthy snack?

64
00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.410
Oh gosh.

65
00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.850
I like to hit all the major food groups.

66
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:42.725
How long do you have?

67
00:02:42.725 --> 00:02:44.380
(laughing)

68
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.943
Let's go back to a basics nutrition course.

69
00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.600
I like to do a protein, a carb and a fat.

70
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.880
And so ideally a whole food from each of those categories.

71
00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:54.713
Okay.

72
00:02:54.713 --> 00:02:57.610
And Brad, what are you giving the kids?

73
00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:01.060
Fruit snacks, Nutter Butters every once in a while.

74
00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.100
You know, things that we have on hand usually.

75
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.950
Yeah, that doesn't sound so bad.

76
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.550
Look, I'm like both of you.

77
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.823
I've got to be, I'm a single mom,

78
00:03:09.737 --> 00:03:10.570
I understand.

79
00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:11.870
I'm two people in one.

80
00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.900
I think that you're both right.

81
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.400
And I think we both tried to do...

82
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.260
By the way I just talked about myself.

83
00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.440
We both, okay?

84
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.793
Do you see what I'm saying?

85
00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.590
The two of me, huh, why, how dare you?

86
00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:26.423
I'm doing good!

87
00:03:26.423 --> 00:03:29.540
No you're not!
(laughing)

88
00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:32.010
There's that style of parenting too.

89
00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:32.843
Look, I get it.

90
00:03:32.843 --> 00:03:34.530
We're all just trying to do the best we can.

91
00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.790
And if we try to find that pretty little middle

92
00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:38.800
within ourselves.

93
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.690
Yes, what, no.

94
00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:45.410
Then, I think life will be a little bit easier.

95
00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:50.410
You guys really have an energy about you that's loving.

96
00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:54.100
And I think if kids also see conflict

97
00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.950
or differences or different styles,

98
00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.830
but they know you can figure out how to resolve it,

99
00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.260
they're gonna figure out that they can go through life,

100
00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.920
which is never without its conflicts

101
00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.320
and know that they can resolve things too.

102
00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.550
Elisa?

103
00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.060
Yes.

104
00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:13.190
Yes. It's not that we want to hide conflict from kids.

105
00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:15.250
It's just that we want to finish the story.

106
00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.410
Like we've got this guys.

107
00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:19.070
We're not in agreement here,

108
00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:20.870
but this is how we resolve it

109
00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:22.200
because then you'd get to your 20s

110
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.287
and you'd fight with someone and think,

111
00:04:23.287 --> 00:04:24.477
"I guess this is it then.

112
00:04:24.477 --> 00:04:26.040
"I've never seen a fight before."

113
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.200
I know.
You know?

114
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.430
Oh please, my parents split before I was born.

115
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:30.263
And I was like,

116
00:04:30.263 --> 00:04:31.927
every time someone would say goodbye, I'd be like,

117
00:04:31.927 --> 00:04:32.857
"Where are you going?!

118
00:04:32.857 --> 00:04:34.167
"To the grocery store?

119
00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:35.567
"You have to make a big deal out of this goodbye,

120
00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:37.107
"it could be the last time!"

121
00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.970
It's not supposed to be sad, I'm great.

122
00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.540
Actually, everything that happened in my life

123
00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.070
has led me to be a really strong person.

124
00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:50.400
And I think that these are some of the most interesting,

125
00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.990
exciting, fun, and important conversations to have in life

126
00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:58.760
because we are all trying to navigate relationships.

127
00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.950
And when it comes to our kids, it's just such high stakes.

128
00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.560
So the stress is totally natural.

129
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.630
Drew, we want to give you

130
00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:07.490
the honorary title of Doctor.

131
00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:09.601
I mean, you're doing really great.

132
00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:10.854
This is awesome.
(audience applauding)

133
00:05:10.854 --> 00:05:11.985
Really?

134
00:05:11.985 --> 00:05:14.390
Yeah, you have lots of great quotes.

135
00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:15.600
Oh my God, thank you.

136
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.420
Well, here's my truth.

137
00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:23.420
Is that I have learned from such smart people,

138
00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:25.890
like this woman who's sitting right here,

139
00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.540
but really I thank you guys for trusting us enough

140
00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:30.720
to come and tell your story

141
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.270
and share your experiences with us.

142
00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.480
And you guys seem to really love each other.

143
00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.960
And it's nice to have that hope, you know?

144
00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.380
Because couples fighting,

145
00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.680
it's so tense and scary for all of us.

146
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.520
And so when you see someone who,

147
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:45.880
you guys do really like each other,

148
00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:47.180
you do really love each other.

149
00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.447
And we're gonna figure this out.

150
00:05:49.447 --> 00:05:50.878
(audience cheering)

151
00:05:50.878 --> 00:05:51.750
Well, it was an honor.

152
00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.030
It was an honor to be here with you.

153
00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:54.820
And honestly, seriously,

154
00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.130
being completely honest, I think this conversation

155
00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:00.380
is changing the trajectory of how,

156
00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:02.470
things have been so heavy lately.

157
00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:05.060
And part of it is our kids' ages

158
00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.780
and we're in the thick of it for sure.

159
00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:11.220
But if we can't figure out how to co-exist as parents,

160
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.550
then I don't know.

161
00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:16.120
And I think, having like this outside view

162
00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:20.720
of our parenting style and our relationship looking in,

163
00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.520
and you guys tell it to us how it looks.

164
00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:25.620
Saying we're both right

165
00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.700
and saying that you can tell we love each other is

166
00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:29.533
I don't know.

167
00:06:29.533 --> 00:06:31.120
We needed this, this was awesome.

168
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.290
Totally.
I'm so glad.

169
00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:34.160
And Elisa, can you also tell them

170
00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.010
the thing about the dinner table?

171
00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.570
Because I, we all try to do that same thing.

172
00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:39.877
Like, it's like,

173
00:06:39.877 --> 00:06:42.737
"Oh, hi, cue the Norman Rockwell painting.

174
00:06:42.737 --> 00:06:43.707
"Aren't we pretty?"

175
00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.500
You get like 10 minutes from a two year old.

176
00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.360
Definitely set the tone.

177
00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.520
We're gonna have our nice dinner conversations,

178
00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:53.500
but for 10 minutes.

179
00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.910
And then every time they have a birthday,

180
00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:57.020
add a little bit of time,

181
00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:00.170
but you're not gonna get a long,

182
00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:01.480
it's never gonna happen.

183
00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.010
So you're just gonna have tension

184
00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:05.310
and you want meal time to be fun.

185
00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:07.170
I just really want to thank you guys again.

186
00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:08.620
And thank you Elisa.

187
00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:09.970
I love you. Thank you.

188
00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:11.120
I love you.

189
00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:12.656
Thank you to everybody watching.

190
00:07:12.656 --> 00:07:15.239
(upbeat music)

